    content: `The resting membrane potential is the electrical voltage that exists across the plasma membrane of a neuron (or any cell) when it is at rest and not signalling. In a typical neuron it is about −70 millivolts (mV), meaning the inside of the cell is 70 mV NEGATIVE relative to the outside. This charge separation is the "battery" that action potentials discharge. It is set up by three things working together: (1) THE SODIUM–POTASSIUM PUMP (Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase) uses ATP to actively transport 3 Na⁺ ions OUT of the cell for every 2 K⁺ ions it brings IN, against their gradients — this keeps the inside K⁺ concentration high (~140 mM) and the inside Na⁺ concentration low (~15 mM), the reverse of outside. (2) SELECTIVE PERMEABILITY — at rest the membrane has many open K⁺ "leak" channels but very few open Na⁺ channels, so it is roughly 25–100× more permeable to K⁺ than to Na⁺. K⁺ therefore diffuses OUT of the cell down its concentration gradient, carrying positive charge out and leaving the inside progressively negative. (3) TRAPPED ANIONS — large negatively charged proteins and phosphates inside the cell cannot cross the membrane. K⁺ keeps leaving until the accumulating negative charge inside becomes strong enough to electrically pull K⁺ back in as hard as the concentration gradient pushes it out — an equilibrium, the "potassium equilibrium potential" of about −90 mV. Because a small amount of Na⁺ does leak inward (and the electrogenic pump contributes a few mV), the actual resting potential settles a little less negative, around −65 to −70 mV. This is why the resting potential is close to, but not exactly, the K⁺ equilibrium potential, and why changing the extracellular K⁺ concentration (as in kidney failure) directly and dangerously alters the excitability of nerve and heart cells.`,
