    content: `Depolarization, repolarization, and hyperpolarization all describe the DIRECTION the membrane voltage is moving relative to the resting membrane potential (about −70 mV in a neuron). DEPOLARIZATION means the membrane potential becomes LESS NEGATIVE — it moves toward 0, and in an action potential it briefly overshoots to about +40 mV (positive inside). It is caused by positively charged ions entering the cell, principally Na⁺ (through voltage-gated sodium channels in an action potential, or through ligand-gated channels at an excitatory synapse), or sometimes Ca²⁺. Depolarization makes a neuron MORE excitable: a small depolarization is a graded "excitatory postsynaptic potential" (EPSP), and if enough EPSPs summate to reach the "threshold" (~−55 mV) at the axon hillock, they trigger a full action potential. Depolarization is the rising (upstroke) phase of the action potential. REPOLARIZATION means the membrane potential returns BACK toward its negative resting value after having been depolarized. It is caused by positive charge leaving the cell — the voltage-gated Na⁺ channels inactivate (stopping the Na⁺ influx) and delayed voltage-gated K⁺ channels open, letting K⁺ flow out down its gradient. Repolarization is the falling (downstroke) phase of the action potential. HYPERPOLARIZATION means the membrane potential becomes even MORE NEGATIVE than the resting potential. In an action potential it occurs as a brief "afterhyperpolarization" or undershoot because the slow K⁺ channels stay open a moment too long. At an inhibitory synapse, hyperpolarization (an "inhibitory postsynaptic potential," IPSP) is produced by GABA or glycine opening Cl⁻ channels (Cl⁻ in) or K⁺ channels (K⁺ out); it moves the cell further from threshold and makes it LESS likely to fire. The interplay of depolarizing (excitatory) and hyperpolarizing (inhibitory) inputs, summed continuously at the cell body, is how a neuron "computes" whether to fire.`,
