    content: `The sodium–potassium pump (Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase) is an enzyme embedded in the plasma membrane of essentially every animal cell — nothing specific to the kidney (though it is important there, as it is everywhere). Each cycle, it binds one molecule of ATP and uses the energy of splitting it to change shape and transport 3 Na⁺ ions OUT of the cell and 2 K⁺ ions IN, both against their concentration gradients (pushing Na⁺ out where Na⁺ is already high, and K⁺ in where K⁺ is already high). Its functions: (1) IT MAINTAINS THE ION GRADIENTS that underlie the RESTING MEMBRANE POTENTIAL and make electrical signalling possible — keeping internal Na⁺ low and internal K⁺ high, ready for the Na⁺ influx of a depolarization and the K⁺ efflux of repolarization. Without it, the gradients run down over minutes and the cell can no longer fire. (2) IT IS ELECTROGENIC — because it exports 3 positive charges for every 2 it imports, it directly contributes a few millivolts of negativity to the resting potential. (3) IT POWERS SECONDARY ACTIVE TRANSPORT — the steep Na⁺ gradient it maintains is a stored energy source that dozens of other membrane carriers tap: the sodium-glucose cotransporters (SGLT) that absorb glucose in the gut and reclaim it in the kidney, the sodium-amino-acid cotransporters, the Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger for pH control, and the Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger that keeps intracellular calcium low. When cardiac glycoside drugs (digoxin) partially block the pump in heart muscle, intracellular Na⁺ rises, the Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger slows, calcium accumulates, and the heartbeat strengthens — the basis of digoxin's use in heart failure. (4) IT REGULATES CELL VOLUME — by constantly bailing out the Na⁺ that leaks in, it prevents the osmotic water entry that would otherwise swell and burst the cell. The pump is metabolically expensive, consuming 20–40% of a typical resting cell's ATP budget and up to 70% in electrically active neurons — a major reason the brain uses so much of the body's energy.`,
