    content: `A NEUROTRANSMITTER is a signalling chemical stored in vesicles at the end of a neuron's axon and released into the synaptic cleft when an action potential arrives (Ca²⁺ influx triggers the vesicles to fuse with the membrane and dump their contents). It diffuses across the ~20 nm gap, binds specific receptors on the target cell's membrane, and briefly changes that cell's membrane potential — making it more likely to fire (excitatory) or less likely (inhibitory) — before being rapidly removed from the cleft by reuptake into the neuron, enzymatic breakdown, or diffusion, so the signal is sharp and short. THE MAIN NEUROTRANSMITTERS: GLUTAMATE — the principal EXCITATORY transmitter of the CNS (used by ~80% of neurons); central to learning and memory (LTP); excess ("excitotoxicity") kills neurons in stroke and head injury. GABA (γ-aminobutyric acid) — the principal INHIBITORY transmitter of the BRAIN; benzodiazepines, alcohol, and general anaesthetics enhance it. GLYCINE — the main inhibitory transmitter of the SPINAL CORD and brainstem. ACETYLCHOLINE (ACh) — the transmitter at the neuromuscular junction (all voluntary muscle contraction), throughout the autonomic ganglia and at parasympathetic endings, and in the brain for attention, arousal, learning and memory (the cholinergic neurons that degenerate in Alzheimer's, which cholinesterase-inhibitor drugs try to compensate for). THE MONOAMINES: DOPAMINE — reward, motivation, reinforcement, and the fine control of movement; the nigrostriatal dopamine neurons die in Parkinson's disease, dopamine surges underlie addiction, and excess dopamine signalling is linked to the psychotic symptoms of schizophrenia. NORADRENALINE (norepinephrine) — arousal, vigilance, attention, stress, and mood. SEROTONIN (5-HT) — mood, sleep, appetite, impulse control, and gut motility; the target of SSRI antidepressants. Also: the NEUROPEPTIDES (endorphins and enkephalins for pain modulation, substance P for pain transmission, oxytocin, orexin for wakefulness, and many others), and unconventional messengers — nitric oxide, endocannabinoids (which signal "backwards" from the postsynaptic cell), adenosine (which caffeine blocks), ATP, and histamine.`,
