    content: `The cerebellum ("little brain") is the wrinkled structure tucked under the back of the cerebrum, behind the brainstem. Although it contains more than half of all the brain's neurons, it does not consciously initiate or plan movement — its job is to MAKE movement work properly. HOW: whenever the motor cortex issues a command to move, it sends a copy ("efference copy") to the cerebellum. The cerebellum simultaneously receives a continuous flood of real-time sensory feedback — proprioception from muscles and joints (where each body part actually is and how fast it's moving), balance and head-position information from the vestibular apparatus of the inner ear, and visual input. It compares the INTENDED movement with the movement that is actually happening, calculates the error, and sends rapid corrective signals (via the thalamus back to the motor cortex, and via the brainstem to the spinal cord) — moment by moment — to keep the movement smooth, accurately aimed, appropriately forceful, and correctly timed. It coordinates the many muscles and joints of a complex action so they act as one. It is also essential for BALANCE and posture, for the vestibulo-ocular reflex (keeping the eyes fixed on a target while the head moves), and for MOTOR LEARNING — acquiring and refining a new skill (riding a bike, a tennis serve) through practice, and then running it automatically. Modern work also implicates it in aspects of language, attention, working memory, and emotional regulation. DAMAGE (stroke, tumour, chronic alcohol use, degenerative ataxias, MS) causes ATAXIA: a broad-based, staggering, "drunken" gait; clumsy, jerky, poorly aimed limb movements ("dysmetria," past-pointing); an "intention tremor" that worsens as the hand approaches a target; slurred, halting "scanning" speech; jerky eye movements (nystagmus); loss of the ability to perform rapid alternating movements (dysdiadochokinesia); and poor balance — but crucially NOT weakness, paralysis, or loss of sensation, since the cerebellum modulates rather than executes.`,
