    content: `The brainstem is the stalk-like lower part of the brain that connects the cerebrum and cerebellum above to the spinal cord below. From bottom to top it has three sections: the MEDULLA OBLONGATA, the PONS, and the MIDBRAIN. Its overriding role is to run the involuntary, automatic functions that keep the body alive from moment to moment. THE MEDULLA houses: the respiratory centre that generates the basic rhythm of breathing (and the chemoreceptors that speed it up when CO₂ rises); the cardiovascular centre that sets the heart rate and, via the vasomotor centre, the diameter of blood vessels and therefore blood pressure; and the reflex centres for coughing, sneezing, swallowing, gagging, vomiting, and hiccuping. THE PONS ("bridge") relays signals between the two cerebral hemispheres and the cerebellum, contributes to the control of breathing (the pneumotaxic and apneustic centres), and is involved in sleep, dreaming (REM), and eye movement, and it originates several cranial nerves (V–VIII). THE MIDBRAIN controls reflex orienting to visual and auditory stimuli (via the superior and inferior colliculi), the pupillary light reflex and the accommodation and eye-movement circuitry, and it contains the substantia nigra (dopamine neurons for movement, degenerating in Parkinson's) and the periaqueductal grey (pain modulation). Running THROUGH the whole brainstem: essentially every ascending sensory tract and descending motor tract between the brain and the body, and it is here — in the medulla — that the main motor pathway (the pyramids) CROSSES OVER ("decussates"), which is why one side of the brain controls the opposite side of the body. Ten of the twelve cranial nerves arise from the brainstem. Spread through its core is the RETICULAR FORMATION and its "reticular activating system," which controls arousal, wakefulness, and the sleep–wake cycle — damage here causes coma. Because the brainstem is so densely packed with vital functions, even a small stroke or bleed there can be devastating (e.g. "locked-in syndrome" — full consciousness but total paralysis except eye movements), and irreversible loss of all brainstem function is the definition of death used for organ donation.`,
