    content: `The hippocampus is a curled, seahorse-shaped strip of cortex ("hippocampus" is Latin for seahorse) buried deep in the medial part of each temporal lobe, alongside the amygdala. Its central function is the FORMATION of new long-term DECLARATIVE (explicit) memories — memories of facts ("semantic memory") and of personally experienced events ("episodic memory"). It works as an indexer and binder: it links together the many separate cortical fragments of an experience (what you saw, heard, felt, where you were, when it was) into a single retrievable memory. Then, over a period of hours, days, and even years — importantly, especially during sleep — the hippocampus repeatedly "replays" and gradually CONSOLIDATES the memory into distributed networks in the neocortex, after which the memory becomes stable and can be recalled without the hippocampus. The evidence: the patient Henry Molaison ("H.M."), who had both hippocampi surgically removed in 1953 to stop severe epilepsy, was left with profound ANTEROGRADE AMNESIA — he could hold a conversation but forgot it minutes later and never formed a single new lasting memory for the rest of his life — yet his childhood memories, his IQ, his personality, his working (short-term) memory, and his ability to learn new motor skills were all intact, revealing that the hippocampus is needed to make new declarative memories but not to store or use old ones or to acquire procedural skills. The hippocampus also constructs a cognitive MAP OF SPACE: it contains "place cells" that each fire only when the animal is in a specific location, and together with "grid cells" in the neighbouring entorhinal cortex they form an internal GPS — the discovery that earned John O'Keefe and May-Britt and Edvard Moser the 2014 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Other notable points: the hippocampus is one of the very few brain regions where new neurons are generated throughout adult life (adult neurogenesis); it is exquisitely vulnerable to oxygen deprivation (so hippocampal damage and memory loss are common after cardiac arrest), to prolonged stress and high cortisol (which can shrink it), and to seizures; and it is one of the FIRST regions to accumulate pathology and shrink in Alzheimer's disease, which is exactly why difficulty forming new memories is the earliest and most characteristic symptom.`,
