    content: `The thalamus is a pair of egg-shaped masses of grey matter sitting at the very centre of the brain, perched on top of the brainstem and enclosed by the cerebral hemispheres (the two halves are usually joined across the midline). It is often called the brain's "relay station" or "gateway to the cortex," and its most important job is SENSORY RELAY AND GATING: with the single exception of smell (which goes straight to the cortex and limbic system), ALL sensory information — vision (to the lateral geniculate nucleus), hearing (medial geniculate nucleus), touch/pain/temperature/proprioception (ventral posterior nucleus), and taste and balance — passes through a dedicated relay nucleus of the thalamus before it is forwarded to the corresponding primary sensory area of the cerebral cortex. But the thalamus is NOT a passive switchboard: each relay nucleus receives even more feedback connections from the cortex than it sends up, and the thalamus actively FILTERS what gets through — turning down irrelevant or repetitive input, focusing on what is being attended to, and closing the sensory gates almost entirely during deep sleep and anaesthesia (which is why you don't wake to normal sounds). Beyond sensory relay, the thalamus also: relays MOTOR signals from the cerebellum and basal ganglia up to the motor and premotor cortex, forming the feedback loops that make movement smooth; contributes to MEMORY (the anterior and mediodorsal nuclei — damage here, as in Korsakoff's syndrome from thiamine deficiency, causes amnesia); participates in AROUSAL, alertness, and CONSCIOUSNESS itself (the intralaminar and reticular nuclei, part of the ascending arousal system — bilateral thalamic damage causes coma); and processes pain and emotion. Because virtually every cortical area has a reciprocal loop with a specific thalamic nucleus, the thalamus is increasingly seen as an active hub that helps orchestrate and synchronise cortex-wide activity, not merely a waystation.`,
