    content: `The hypothalamus is a small region — roughly the size of an almond, under 1% of the brain's mass — at the base of the brain, sitting below the thalamus (hence "hypo-") and directly above the pituitary gland, to which it is joined by a short stalk (the infundibulum). Despite its size it is the brain's principal regulator of HOMEOSTASIS (keeping the body's internal conditions stable) and the crucial bridge between the NERVOUS SYSTEM and the ENDOCRINE SYSTEM. Its functions, run by a set of small paired nuclei: (1) TEMPERATURE — it is the body's thermostat, sensing blood temperature and driving shivering, sweating, vasoconstriction or vasodilation, and behavioural responses to hold core temperature near 37 °C, and it produces fever by raising the set-point. (2) HUNGER AND SATIETY — it integrates hormonal signals (leptin from fat, insulin, ghrelin from the stomach, gut hormones) and nutrient levels to generate the drives to eat and to stop eating; damage causes obesity or wasting. (3) THIRST AND WATER BALANCE — osmoreceptors here detect blood concentration and trigger thirst, and its neurons make ADH (vasopressin) to control kidney water reabsorption. (4) CIRCADIAN RHYTHM AND SLEEP — the suprachiasmatic nucleus is the body's master ~24-hour clock, entrained to the light–dark cycle via the retina, and it governs the sleep–wake cycle, the daily cortisol and body-temperature rhythms, and the timing of melatonin release from the pineal. (5) ENDOCRINE CONTROL — it secretes "releasing" and "inhibiting" hormones (CRH, TRH, GnRH, GHRH, somatostatin, dopamine) into the tiny portal blood vessels running to the anterior pituitary, thereby commanding the thyroid, adrenal, gonadal, and growth-hormone axes; and its magnocellular neurons synthesise ADH and OXYTOCIN, which are transported down their axons and released from the posterior pituitary. (6) AUTONOMIC AND EMOTIONAL/BEHAVIOURAL CONTROL — it is the head ganglion of the autonomic nervous system (coordinating sympathetic and parasympathetic output), it drives the physiological "fight-or-flight" stress response, and it governs primitive drives and behaviours including aggression, fear, sexual behaviour, and — via oxytocin and vasopressin — social bonding and attachment.`,
