    content: `The cerebrum is divided into two hemispheres, left and right, which look like near-mirror images and are connected across the midline by a massive bundle of ~200 million axons, the CORPUS CALLOSUM, plus smaller commissures, through which they exchange information continuously and work together on virtually everything. (This is about the brain — it is not about the left and right LUNG.) There are two robust and universal asymmetries: (1) CONTRALATERAL CONTROL — each hemisphere controls the voluntary movement of, and receives the touch, pain, and temperature sensation from, the OPPOSITE side of the body (because the main pathways cross in the brainstem/spinal cord), and each processes the opposite half of the visual field. So a stroke in the left hemisphere weakens the right arm and leg, and vice versa. (2) LANGUAGE LATERALISATION — in about 95% of right-handed people and around 70% of left-handed people, the LEFT hemisphere is dominant for language: Broca's area (speech production) and Wernicke's area (comprehension) are on the left, which is why left-hemisphere strokes commonly cause aphasia. The left hemisphere also tends to be somewhat better at sequential, analytical, detail-focused, and arithmetic/logical processing. The RIGHT hemisphere tends to be relatively specialised for spatial attention and navigation, recognising faces and objects, appreciating music and the emotional tone ("prosody") of speech, understanding humour and metaphor, and holistic ("big picture") pattern processing; right-hemisphere damage can cause "neglect" (ignoring the left side of space). HOWEVER — the popular notion that people are "left-brained" (logical, mathematical) or "right-brained" (creative, artistic) as a personality type is a MYTH with no scientific support: neuroimaging shows both hemispheres are engaged in nearly all complex tasks, creativity draws on the whole brain, and there is no evidence individuals preferentially use one side. The genuine specialisations are subtle relative biases within a tightly integrated system. The clearest demonstrations of hemispheric specialisation come from "split-brain" patients (whose corpus callosum was surgically severed to control intractable epilepsy): under carefully controlled lab conditions where a stimulus is shown to only one visual field, the two hemispheres can be seen to "know" different things and even disagree — but in everyday life these patients behave almost normally.`,
