    content: `An action potential ("nerve impulse," "spike") is a brief, stereotyped, self-regenerating reversal of the membrane voltage that sweeps along an axon at up to ~120 m/s, carrying a signal from the cell body to the axon terminals. It is ALL-OR-NOTHING: a stimulus either reaches threshold and fires a full-sized action potential, or it doesn't fire at all — the amplitude never varies, so information is encoded in the FREQUENCY of firing (and the pattern), not the size. THE SEQUENCE, at one point on the axon: (1) RESTING STATE — the membrane sits at about −70 mV, with voltage-gated Na⁺ and K⁺ channels closed. (2) DEPOLARISATION TO THRESHOLD — an incoming signal (summed excitatory synaptic input, or the impulse arriving from the previous patch of axon) depolarises the membrane; if it reaches the THRESHOLD of about −55 mV, the process becomes self-sustaining. (3) RISING PHASE (rapid depolarisation) — voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open in a fraction of a millisecond, Na⁺ pours into the cell down its steep electrochemical gradient, driving the membrane potential rapidly upward and overshooting to about +30 to +40 mV (inside now positive). The Na⁺ influx itself depolarises neighbouring membrane, opening its Na⁺ channels — this is what propagates the impulse. (4) FALLING PHASE (repolarisation) — after ~1 ms the Na⁺ channels automatically INACTIVATE (a separate gate closes, stopping the influx), and the slower voltage-gated K⁺ channels finish opening; K⁺ now flows OUT of the cell, carrying positive charge out and driving the potential back down toward and past the resting value. (5) AFTERHYPERPOLARISATION (undershoot) — the K⁺ channels close slowly, so for a few milliseconds the membrane is briefly MORE negative than rest (~−80 mV), before the resting state is restored and the Na⁺/K⁺ pump quietly re-balances the ions. (6) REFRACTORY PERIOD — during the "absolute refractory period" (the rising phase and early falling phase) the Na⁺ channels are open or inactivated and NO new action potential can be triggered no matter how strong the stimulus; during the following "relative refractory period" (the afterhyperpolarisation) a stronger-than-normal stimulus is needed. The refractory period does two vital things: it forces the impulse to travel in ONE direction only (the patch just behind cannot re-fire), and it sets a ceiling on how rapidly a neuron can fire (typically up to a few hundred Hz). In myelinated axons, the action potential regenerates only at the gaps in the myelin (nodes of Ranvier), jumping from node to node — "saltatory conduction" — which is far faster and more energy-efficient.`,
